#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139330} :activity/id #uuid "a31e6d9a-a5fd-5a92-8bfe-ccf7c139d6c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049369 :topic/id #uuid "ef52c4c1-b1f3-57d2-9328-5e8e0f981eaa" :topic/math-academy-id 2273} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139718 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're making a juice drink with a fixed amount of pure orange and the rest water. \n\nThe more water that we add, the less that we'll be able to taste the orange. \n\nMore precisely, the concentration of pure orange $y$ (in grams per liter) varies **inversely** with the volume $x$ (in liters) of water added. We can express this idea using an **inverse variation equation** as follows:\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{k}{x}\n$$\n\nThe constant $k$ is called the **constant of variation** or **constant of proportionality.**\n\nNotice that:\n\n- If $x$ is small (i.e., there is not much water), then $y$ is large (i.e., there is a high concentration of orange).\n\n- If $x$ is large (i.e., there is lots of water), then $y$ is small (i.e., there is a low concentration of orange).\n\nTo find the constant of variation $k$, we need to plug in some data. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that the concentration is $y = 200\\, \\textrm{g/L}$ when $x = 4\\,\\textrm L$ of water has been added. Plugging this into our relationship and solving for $k,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{k}{x} \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{k}{4} \\\\ 200\\cdot 4 & =k \\\\ 800 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relationship between $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {800} x.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that we can rewrite the above relationship as\n\n$$\nxy = 800.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "119903a2-f337-5919-9085-bff5477e0375" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8754 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b9c91899-d231-53dd-a5ef-3ff5dda6d1ff" :step/math-academy-id 191249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138169 :question/id #uuid "8a12b2fd-6361-59ef-8378-6c900a1ab47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14089"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498567}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"} :step/id #uuid "7536e83b-56d0-53cd-8fc7-4e96012da74b" :step/math-academy-id 191250 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139332}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138414 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138170 :question/id #uuid "a2d2a2a1-da7b-5106-8347-3a4f71ddd619" :question/math-academy-id "e-14090"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e239c25-66f9-5ea7-ba3c-5599966cd89b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518113}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Data Point Given Another Data Point"} :step/id #uuid "c2dcaebf-65a1-5edf-9cd3-fd75eb90fc4b" :step/math-academy-id 191254 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139720 :tutorial/content "If  $y$ varies inversely with  $x,$ then we can also say that $y$ is **inversely proportional** to $x.$ We can express this mathematically as\n\n$$\ny \\propto \\dfrac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\propto$ is the proportionality symbol.\n\nThe following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{k}{x}$ for some constant $k$\n\n- $y$ varies inversely with $x$\n\n- $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto \\dfrac{1}{x}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5b87278f-f657-5fcd-a760-5c4b53c783b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8756 :tutorial/title "Alternative Formulations"} :step/id #uuid "53ccf363-f93a-5e86-87e9-b5eba1af0a5c" :step/math-academy-id 191258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138415 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138172 :question/id #uuid "80333f8b-9aff-5f31-8301-a8708d531a22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14092"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c4d2228-2456-536c-87f5-c05f03ac1532" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518108}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents an Inverse Variation"} :step/id #uuid "a384bde8-822b-5921-a245-47c8bf6118d6" :step/math-academy-id 191259 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138416 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138173 :question/id #uuid "9c285374-0465-52ed-a283-c469e123d167" :question/math-academy-id "e-14094"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "482e3ce6-1938-5bbe-a0d3-2c44213c7746" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048993}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518200}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3663f818-19c8-5a2e-a534-3e4acaee2552" :step/math-academy-id 191264}] :activity/title "Inverse Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138599}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518308} :activity/id #uuid "2fbef69a-88c5-5d96-acb5-ba687d9fd119" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049369 :topic/id #uuid "ef52c4c1-b1f3-57d2-9328-5e8e0f981eaa" :topic/math-academy-id 2273} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff8e18-e1e0-569c-b239-ea2d323c2f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517506} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3f1cdb-62a7-5446-bfc0-9c5b8ecff4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517570} {:answer/id #uuid "37c06ed4-4dac-5a25-bb1d-90e1da0780f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517598} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5ad2d6-7a2b-58fa-9b9e-b5cfbe4b0f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517610} {:answer/id #uuid "72cc3a7f-05b3-50d0-8c4f-4aedc32143ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517708} :answer-field/id #uuid "d155659c-c17e-5eca-b3d0-23cecfde70bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf708341-904e-56d2-b929-e74c203d19a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115589" :question/problem "![](images/96/9674072fe2d9f831635dfd2994b97ea6e9603dd576586f80e4b1b2e6a2373564.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x,$ find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 & =k \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation that relates $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2})}{x}=\\frac{5}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we also have that $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ when $x=a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2a} \\\\ 10a & =10 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "47c6ba42-9469-510d-8375-d74fdf459167" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e2c107-9d5e-504f-af80-17ffca1d2a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517858} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f79e97-d630-5908-a05a-ee3b6a7eb854" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1c37fb77-7c99-58f3-b32e-45223dbee74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-217248" :question/problem "Suppose that $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ Then, if $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{k}{12} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 & =k \\\\ 9 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{9}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =9÷(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "946d1bf9-981b-5a6b-afb7-e0f580f11f36"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1039e812-9a88-5b9b-b551-269c2393b23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517522} {:answer/id #uuid "763b9117-6856-5174-865d-697d35865dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ does not vary inversely with $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517712} {:answer/id #uuid "aef8d086-e3c3-5c2f-b73f-df730fbb213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517817} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b86d47-b504-5381-aaca-f8c9b8bcb39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517837} {:answer/id #uuid "f07b6d5a-a5a6-55bc-bf86-f6c9b061a44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517817} :answer-field/id #uuid "f89a1044-5822-5030-9b03-184a93ebec0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11ab4bcc-ff94-5b73-9790-5edd61c08193" :question/math-academy-id "q-115488" :question/problem "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ | $0.5\\cdot 20=10$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2\\cdot 5=10$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ | $4\\cdot 2.5=10$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=10.$ Therefore, $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=10.$"} :step/id #uuid "e3cb0b4d-15dc-5baa-9658-0e5055d4371c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518249}}] :activity/title "Inverse Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517480}]}
